# iolbox macOS/Lima pin set
#
# This file is intentionally shell-sourceable rather than YAML.  The macOS
# entry point sources it and renders the Lima template at run time, so the
# image digest has one authoritative home.  Keep the assignments simple:
# callers may safely use `. packaging/macos/lima/pinned-image.env`.
#
# The URL is the qualified Ubuntu 22.04 arm64 cloud image from the release
# directory recorded in the M1 contract.  Do not replace it with
# template://ubuntu-22.04: Lima's template alias is resolved to whatever
# image a later Lima/template release considers current, so it is not stable
# over time and cannot reproduce the Rosetta/kernel qualification.
#
# To re-pin deliberately, choose a new Ubuntu release directory, fetch its
# SHA256SUMS, and copy the checksum for the exact image named by the URL into
# IOLBOX_JAMMY_IMAGE_SHA256.  For example, compare the downloaded image with
# `shasum -a 256` (or `sha256sum`) and the matching SHA256SUMS line before
# changing either assignment.  Re-run the full M1 qualification after a
# re-pin; a checksum proves bytes, not Rosetta compatibility.
#
# These are the explicit geometry defaults from the M0 hardware record
# (docs/macos-m0-result.md:23-32): 4 vCPUs, 4 GiB RAM, and a 15 GiB disk.
# They are explicit because Lima/host defaults vary by machine and version.
# The machine name is also kept here so the launcher and documentation share a
# stable default while callers can still select another Lima instance through
# IOLBOX_MACHINE when needed.

IOLBOX_JAMMY_IMAGE_URL=https://cloud-images.ubuntu.com/releases/22.04/release-20260807/ubuntu-22.04-server-cloudimg-arm64.img
IOLBOX_JAMMY_IMAGE_SHA256=b17d9ac9b6249ab30f8c95630acdab3b7a51d76050229ab0ce6c013e303f5ccd
IOLBOX_CPUS=4
IOLBOX_MEMORY=4GiB
IOLBOX_DISK=15GiB
IOLBOX_MACHINE_NAME=iolbox
